test_ao_scan
============

An example of how an extension can read ao_row and ao_column tables by
itself, without a tuple slot and a call for every row, through the public
functions the core has for that:

  - appendonly_scan_next_block, AppendOnlyExecutorReadBlock_NextTuple,
    AppendOnlyExecutorReadBlock_BindingInit and memtuple_getattr_inline
    for ao_row;
  - aocs_scan_next_seg, datumstreamread_block, datumstreamread_advance and
    datumstreamread_get for ao_column.

test_ao_scan.c gives the rows of a block at once, up to 64 of them, column
by column, with a mask of the rows the snapshot sees. test_ao_scan_compare
reads a table both this way and through tuple slots and checks that the two
agree on every value, so the tests also keep these functions working.

The functions called for every row or value are inline in the headers, as
a module outside the backend can inline nothing else. A value passed by
value comes in the Datum fetch_att makes of it, which the test checks too.

Run the tests against a running cluster:

    make install
    make installcheck
